/**
 * The headline metrics for one simulation run, with their denominators and censoring made
 * explicit.
 *
 * **Why the field names are so pedantic.** Waiting-time comparisons across plans are the metric
 * most easily made to lie. If a candidate plan serves more people, the extra people it serves are
 * typically the ones who waited longest — so an average over *completed* passengers alone can
 * improve while the day gets worse, or worsen while it gets better. This type therefore reports
 * three things side by side and refuses to collapse them:
 *
 * 1. completed-passenger average and p95, over {@link SimMetrics#completedPassengers};
 * 2. a horizon-based total ({@link SimMetrics#horizonTotalWaitingMinutes}) that *includes* wait
 *    accrued by passengers who never boarded;
 * 3. the unserved counts themselves ({@link SimMetrics#unservedAtHorizon}).
 *
 * Accrued wait is a **lower bound**, not an eventual wait, and is labelled as such.
 *
 * **Lateness.** The denominator is {@link SimMetrics#deadlineBearingPassengers} — only passengers
 * who actually have a deadline. Completed-but-late is reported separately from
 * deadline-bearing-but-unserved: an unserved passenger is never counted as on time, and never
 * silently folded into the late count either.
 *
 * **Savings.** Computed as `baseline - candidate` on identical definitions, and **not clamped**. A
 * candidate that performs worse than baseline shows a negative saving, because hiding that would
 * defeat the purpose of the comparison.
 */
type SimMetrics {

  /**
   * Mean wait, in minutes, over passengers who **completed** their journey. Denominator is
   * {@link SimMetrics#completedPassengers}; read together with
   * {@link SimMetrics#unservedAtHorizon}, never alone.
   */
  avgWaitMinutesCompleted: double

  /**
   * 95th-percentile wait in minutes over completed passengers, by nearest-rank on the
   * passenger-weighted distribution. Exposes the tail that an average flattens.
   */
  p95WaitMinutesCompleted: double

  /** Total wait in minutes accumulated by completed passengers only. */
  totalWaitingMinutesCompleted: double

  /**
   * Total wait in minutes over **all** generated passengers: completed waits plus the wait accrued
   * up to {@link SimScenario#horizonEnd} by passengers still queued.
   *
   * This is the comparison-safe aggregate — it cannot be improved by abandoning passengers. The
   * accrued portion is censored at the horizon and is a lower bound on any eventual wait.
   */
  horizonTotalWaitingMinutes: double

  /** The accrued-but-incomplete portion of {@link SimMetrics#horizonTotalWaitingMinutes}, broken out. */
  accruedWaitingMinutesUnserved: double

  /** Passengers who completed their journey — the denominator for the completed-passenger statistics. */
  completedPassengers: int

  /**
   * Waiting minutes saved versus the mock old-model baseline, on the
   * {@link SimMetrics#horizonTotalWaitingMinutes} definition: `baseline - candidate`.
   *
   * **Negative values are preserved**, never clamped to zero. Null on the baseline run itself,
   * which has nothing to compare against.
   */
  horizonWaitingMinutesSavedVsBaseline: double

  /** Mean total trip time (wait plus in-vehicle), in minutes, over completed passengers. */
  avgTripTimeMinutesCompleted: double

  /** Mean in-vehicle time in minutes over completed passengers. */
  avgInVehicleMinutesCompleted: double

  /**
   * Passengers who had a deadline, completed their journey, and arrived **after** it. Strictly a
   * subset of {@link SimMetrics#completedPassengers}.
   */
  lateCompletedPassengers: int

  /**
   * Passengers carrying a deadline at all — the **only** valid lateness denominator. Equals
   * {@link SimDemandSnapshot#deadlineBearingPassengers} and is therefore identical across baseline
   * and candidates.
   */
  deadlineBearingPassengers: int

  /**
   * {@link SimMetrics#lateCompletedPassengers} as a percentage of
   * {@link SimMetrics#deadlineBearingPassengers}, 0..100. Null when the denominator is zero, rather
   * than a misleading 0%.
   */
  lateCompletedPct: double

  /**
   * Deadline-bearing passengers who never arrived at all — unserved, so neither on time nor
   * "late".
   *
   * Reported separately and deliberately **not** added to
   * {@link SimMetrics#lateCompletedPassengers}, because conflating "arrived late" with "never
   * arrived" would let a plan look punctual by stranding people.
   */
  deadlineBearingUnserved: int

  /**
   * Passengers who had not reached their destination when the horizon ended: still waiting, still
   * onboard, or denied by a stop closure. See {@link SimAccounting} for the breakdown.
   */
  unservedAtHorizon: int

  /** Total extra-bus operating cost for the run, in {@link SimMetrics#currency}. Zero for the baseline. */
  extraBusOperatingCost: double

  /** ISO 4217 code for {@link SimMetrics#extraBusOperatingCost}, from {@link SimCostConfig#currency}. */
  currency: string

  /**
   * {@link SimMetrics#extraBusOperatingCost} divided by
   * {@link SimMetrics#horizonWaitingMinutesSavedVsBaseline}.
   *
   * **Null whenever savings are zero or negative** — a cost per minute saved is meaningless when
   * no minutes were saved, and reporting a negative or infinite ratio would be worse than
   * reporting nothing. {@link SimMetrics#costPerMinuteSavedUnavailableReason} explains which case
   * applies so the UI can say why the cell is empty.
   */
  costPerWaitingMinuteSaved: double

  /**
   * Why {@link SimMetrics#costPerWaitingMinuteSaved} is null: `BaselineRun`, `ZeroSavings`,
   * `NegativeSavings`, or null when the ratio is available.
   */
  costPerMinuteSavedUnavailableReason: string

  /**
   * Restatement of what went into the cost: whether idle allocated buses were charged
   * ({@link SimCostConfig#includeIdleAllocatedBuses}), and that operating, deadhead and dwell
   * minutes are all included while core-service cost is excluded.
   *
   * Travels with the metrics so a cost figure is never read without its basis.
   */
  costBasisNote: string

  /**
   * Plain-language statement of the denominators and of the horizon censoring, rendered next to
   * the metrics in the UI and included in exports.
   */
  denominatorNote: string
}
